<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Taizong of Tang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/626">626</a>–649</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Li Shimin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Taizong, born Li Shimin, was the second emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> (r. 626–649). He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a>, expanded Tang power into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and consolidated the institutions of government. He is remembered as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s greatest rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang.jpg" alt="Emperor Taizong of Tang" width="203" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TangTaizong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Goguryeo's defences met their greatest test in the wars with Sui and Tang: Sui Wendi's expedition of 598 failed, Sui Yangdi's huge army of 612 was routed by Eulji Mundeok at the Salsu River, and in 645 <b>Tang Taizong</b> took Liaodong fortress but abandoned the siege of Ansi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the dynasty's last years rebellion drew able-bodied men away from the farms; some deliberately broke their limbs to avoid conscription, calling it 'propitious paws' and 'fortunate feet', a practice <b>Emperor Taizong of Tang</b> was still trying to stamp out in 642.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tang founding was a family enterprise: Li Yuan's second son, <b>Li Shimin</b>, played the decisive military role in the campaigns that secured the dynasty, and in 626 staged the Xuanwu Gate Incident, an ambush in which he killed his brothers, the crown prince Li Jiancheng and Li…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 634 he sent his first embassy to Tang China, and after a Tibetan attack on the Tang frontier at Songzhou in 638 the emperor <b>Taizong</b> agreed to a marriage alliance: Princess Wencheng arrived in Tibet in 641.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Li Shimin</b> ambushed and killed his brothers Li Jiancheng and Li Yuanji at the Xuanwu Gate of Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tang prince <b>Li Shimin</b>, besieging Wang Shichong in Luoyang, held the Hulao Pass against Dou Jiande's relieving Xia army, routed it and captured Dou.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-28">28 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
